      "writtenOn": "2021-03-08T02:07:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think this is convenient, but at the same time makes the debugging difficult because the test components are reordered behind the scene. Can\u0027t we just checkTestOrder even in that case?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 234,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "23ea53c2b5a40868b8b3abbf16da9dfcaa496ec7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d131223_ccf77c46",
        "filename": "android/testing.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 235,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-08T09:31:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I initially just checked the test order but there were lots of tests that failed. So I tried reordering them and only got one failure. So, checking the order seemed like a worse solution because it not only places the burden of ensuring the correct order on the developer but also causes tests that are insensitive to the differences in the order to fail.\n\nDebugging might be a little more confusing if the developer does not know that they are being reordered or what the runtime ordering is supposed to be but I think on the whole it is better to be debugging a test that accurately represents the runtime behavior than one that does not.\n\nI have been thinking that one way to address the debugging issue is to allow easier introspection of the state of the system, e.g. maybe add a Dump() method that just prints the state (including mutator/singleton/pre-singleton order). So, someone who has been debugging a weird test failure can simply add a call to the method which will dump the state out.\n\nOr maybe create a structure that gathers all the information into one place for inspection via the debugger.",
      "parentUuid": "eacd78c9_77fed69e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 234,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "23ea53c2b5a40868b8b3abbf16da9dfcaa496ec7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "662a7b1f_d2d42035",
        "filename": "android/testing.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 235,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-08T11:39:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, I don\u0027t disagree what you said.\n\nActually, what I really wanted to have is the ability to specify dependencies among the mutators. (e.g. this mutator should execute before X and after Y and Z) Then, regardless of the registration order, soong will decide the total order of the mutators according to the dependencies. Of course the tie-breaking rule should be consistent and deterministic.\n\nIf we think such an automatic ordering is the way to go (eventually), I would feel more comfortable about reordering the test mutators which this CL does.",
      "parentUuid": "5d131223_ccf77c46",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 234,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "23ea53c2b5a40868b8b3abbf16da9dfcaa496ec7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8230f576_ccd93731",
        "filename": "android/testing.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 235,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-08T12:04:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I actually had a change https://r.android.com/1612945 that would do this for the new test fixture model I have been working on but when I tried to convert existing tests to use it I realized that it was very difficult to work with as order of initialization of packages, as well as order of initialization of each of the files within the package, play a large role in defining the order.\n\nThat is when I realized that the simplest way to ensure consistent ordering was to make tests use the same order as the runtime.\n\nI have given it some thought and it should be possible, although I have no plans at the moment, to modify the fixture graph that I used to do the topological sort to do something similar with mutators. I think that the mutators would need to be identified by name (which would require their names to be defined as constants for reference from elsewhere. Also, I am not sure if the topological sort as currently implemented is stable enough.\n\nThat sorting would only need to be applied to the mutators (and singletons) used at runtime as this change will enforce the same ordering on most tests. The exceptions are a couple of namespace tests that add a custom \"rename\" mutator.",
      "parentUuid": "662a7b1f_d2d42035",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 234,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "23ea53c2b5a40868b8b3abbf16da9dfcaa496ec7",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "dd4769cb_df7586b0",
        "filename": "android/testing.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 235,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-03-08T13:24:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e order of initialization of packages, as well as order of initialization of each of the files within the package, play a large role in defining the order\n\nYes, that\u0027s what I thought makes the ordering inflexible or difficult to play with. It\u0027s sometimes impossible (or very tricky) to insert a specific mutator at a certain point, especially when the initialization order does not match with the data dependency among mutators.\n\nSo, when a pair of mutators don\u0027t have dependencies specified among them, we could by default use the initialization order to order them as we do today. But if dependencies are specified, it shall override the initialization order.",
      "parentUuid": "8230f576_ccd93731",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 234,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 235,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "23ea53c2b5a40868b8b3abbf16da9dfcaa496ec7",
